Here's an example C++ program that reads a text file line by line and counts the number of occurrences of a specific word in the file:

```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <string>
#include <algorithm>

int main() {
    // Set the file name and word to search for
    std::string fileName = "input.txt";
    std::string searchWord = "example";

    // Open the file for reading
    std::ifstream inputFile(fileName);
    if (!inputFile) {
        std::cerr << "Error opening file: " << fileName << std::endl;
        return 1;
    }

    // Read the file line by line
    int wordCount = 0;
    std::string line;
    while (getline(inputFile, line)) {
        // Convert the line to lowercase for case-insensitive search
        std::transform(line.begin(), line.end(), line.begin(), ::tolower);

        // Count the number of occurrences of the search word
        size_t pos = 0;
        while ((pos = line.find(searchWord, pos)) != std::string::npos) {
            wordCount++;
            pos += searchWord.length();
        }
    }

    // Close the file
    inputFile.close();

    // Output the word count
    std::cout << "The word \"" << searchWord << "\" appears " << wordCount << " times in " << fileName << "." << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

Make sure to replace `input.txt` with the actual name of your input file, and `example` with the word you want to search for.

This program uses the `ifstream` class to open the input file, the `getline` function to read the file line by line, the `transform` function to convert each line to lowercase for case-insensitive search, and the `find` function to search for the specified word in each line. The `pos` variable keeps track of the current position in the line where the next occurrence of the search word is found.

Note that this program assumes that the search word does not contain any whitespace characters. If your search word can contain whitespace, you will need to modify the `find` function accordingly. Also, this program only counts exact matches of the search word, not partial matches. If you want to count partial matches, you may need to modify the `find` function further.
